#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/id #uuid "9d70bf5e-9f4f-5e73-8105-c3273eb9e735" :question/math-academy-id "e-8852" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2x^3-8x^2+8x$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-{2}^{4}+\\frac{4\\cdot {2}^{3}}{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{2}+\\frac{{2}^{5}}{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{5}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot 32(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "b28d5323-bb98-5eb9-b08f-f7ea663498e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8854" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=3x-x^2$ and $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=3x-x^2$ and $g(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 3x-{x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ 3x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/5a/5a9fd40682646575ada14297ef7e1de766852f99dbdab986d5baa1fb5c2feb94.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=\\dfrac12 x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=3x-x^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be calculated as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([3x-{x}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{2x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\pi [(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{4})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41244e00-b150-5b4c-8638-702096045e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540637} {:answer/id #uuid "452c66bd-c08b-56d2-9072-2aeb65eb8298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540645} {:answer/id #uuid "64b14c65-c2b8-50cd-bfff-c9542b88a423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540699} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d2a100-054d-5a3c-9a3f-d0f54096b329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{4})}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540838} {:answer/id #uuid "c60ba7d1-1858-5df8-80f7-64228bd8df41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c60ba7d1-1858-5df8-80f7-64228bd8df41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540859} :answer-field/id #uuid "554543f4-45d4-5a55-baae-0ae37bd43f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6674be9e-fa9a-51c5-9fa3-be41c5ad9ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-139183" :question/problem "![](images/17/179894a17a2aadf5ba9f463ae3faf2e5836f7b268066f8f56de0f3cf3ff2d8fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c497e8-eb24-5f03-a516-20786f265c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540531} {:answer/id #uuid "18637853-4229-5999-8b4d-c1dd1a465baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540564} {:answer/id #uuid "38029232-7759-563f-8848-6bd142a2cee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540616} {:answer/id #uuid "39b0f50f-1e37-54c3-b540-0f1c1859c060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540621} {:answer/id #uuid "52879a9f-1bc1-5320-8ad2-85852b163ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52879a9f-1bc1-5320-8ad2-85852b163ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540667} :answer-field/id #uuid "f590a07e-b055-5cd3-bfa4-36805a56228e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce012b33-e22a-528a-bd43-8ae57ebb53bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139184" :question/problem "![](images/51/51143fcae2b39d210e9274fd26363dfdc5cc72e289aea9f524f34f0e92b82b09.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23626433-e7c8-5f34-b75a-b0a3c5d7ac80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540589} {:answer/id #uuid "702bdb29-aee6-59b6-969c-a02baad1c793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540722} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7684e8-8b8b-5398-bdb0-2e5e5a3dc1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540793} {:answer/id #uuid "cec76fe3-d4f9-5320-849b-0dfb2d9982c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540876} {:answer/id #uuid "e47089a9-82ba-5d9c-ae70-534d9d331831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e7684e8-8b8b-5398-bdb0-2e5e5a3dc1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540793} :answer-field/id #uuid "59f2f9fb-5153-5952-bf13-386d35bef9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6488b4-82a0-5ac0-9b15-2d4fb9f9b1f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139211" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5b69213924ffd4f7aef6acf1837fd05fbf6b0fd684de12a19f067e452fe42ea.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4} \\\\ 4x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(2-x)(2+x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/cc/ccbaa663f6f108322f7bafd4a192a5b9acdf913b61b8319048e92b7885458a91.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([x]-[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45835387-0c1c-5d4b-8457-ff714a80c9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540647} {:answer/id #uuid "66549397-7ffc-5509-bf4a-c170eda19f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540701} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f04dfc-2a73-5512-bd3a-e55c9fef4431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540797} {:answer/id #uuid "a4049b8a-8825-5669-9caa-a14e90d86938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540802} {:answer/id #uuid "e80076d7-c03b-5639-b30a-dfb3cf2ac755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2x-{x}^{4})}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45835387-0c1c-5d4b-8457-ff714a80c9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540647} :answer-field/id #uuid "43ca6906-7838-5f5e-b687-6a0de5308bd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4022977e-c101-551c-847a-d892e1fa6f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139260" :question/problem "![](images/11/11b85e35604a0350a9fd7754dfda481000debb20397d9aa593a1b887409a97c7.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}$ and $y=2x,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}$ and $g(x)=2x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =2x \\\\ 8x-{x}^{3}-2x & =0 \\\\ 4x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(2-x)(2+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/e0/e03c88c1d24116cc9f68c69150eba39cb6593b4bf6fd4ca9640987ce4ba62299.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]-[2x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb55645-fb6e-544b-b276-80013b78e710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540547} {:answer/id #uuid "2392997a-9561-5830-b7b7-5b120afb733d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540590} {:answer/id #uuid "4b01f3af-a426-5893-b342-d89aa273edf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540662} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa3aea5-a4b1-58bb-afec-e41b2aa01602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540691} {:answer/id #uuid "b6506ccf-88db-5615-8143-2e08863adf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa3aea5-a4b1-58bb-afec-e41b2aa01602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540691} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d1f004-828d-5d62-932b-959a70b00f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4cf3168-db28-57ab-9733-9a47f7b36ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139282" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $y=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =-x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/27/27919b5cbe7c0d8b7f7c58a9378f5aa3d30e5466c63b1b17d8a1b6af028ecf66.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x([2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]-[-x+\\frac{5}{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}(-{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+2{x}^{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{625}{4}+250-\\frac{125}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{4}+2-\\frac{5}{2})] \\\\  & =\\pi [\\frac{125}{4}-(-\\frac{3}{4})] \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f0467a-b008-57da-a9a3-fe734f1b4c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540536} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7fe586-ba60-562a-b399-5620cba64770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540579} {:answer/id #uuid "2805feae-de51-573f-a529-44b89f3883c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540598} {:answer/id #uuid "3d299622-ea68-58f5-af2a-d8dd193d6a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{225\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540630} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9ea190-4206-5d9f-aa9e-7a3d7f4efd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a9ea190-4206-5d9f-aa9e-7a3d7f4efd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540786} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece1f034-27d4-5b04-b263-6e6fc7866f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d5c9e76-b23d-5f4b-b9c9-3236b501399a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139295" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}-3x.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=2x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}-3x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x & ={x}^{2}-3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ x(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=5.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=5.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/d8/d8833c0c6f1becdbe2841f11fae25bb8e558edd06abee6668b2593c4e37c747b.png)\n\n  \n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}-3x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}x([2x]-[{x}^{2}-3x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}x(5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{{5}^{4}}{3}-\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2bfa8ff-2170-5808-88ce-f923ae4b22b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bfa8ff-2170-5808-88ce-f923ae4b22b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540799} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a634dcb-2ecf-54f4-96f1-be7f5d63e9a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0dfc8af8-b45c-5404-a021-aa0515c46816" :question/math-academy-id "q-352254" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-4+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=4.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x(-4+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-4x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi ((-2({4}^{2})+\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}({4}^{4}))-(-2+\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{45}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed820-a9a3-5250-8205-8b2479f31381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed820-a9a3-5250-8205-8b2479f31381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540815} :answer-field/id #uuid "16743b63-9c0b-507f-81e5-a93679be7ea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0beb016-ee67-53e9-a1ae-c396296738c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352258" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-6+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=2$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}x(-6+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(-6x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-3(3)^{2}+\\frac{5(3)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4})-(-3(2)^{2}+\\frac{5(2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aed6510-bb09-5e6b-ae25-17e7bdfaba11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{512\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aed6510-bb09-5e6b-ae25-17e7bdfaba11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{512\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540762} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5b4fa0-79f8-593b-a77f-39924f98f32a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "845bcab8-f4ff-59be-a2f2-b0d273cf58d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-352264" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(4-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=4.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}x(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {({x}^{4}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [({4}^{4}-\\frac{{4}^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (256-\\frac{1024}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{512\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820ac9df-49d1-590e-bc4c-409a7349c4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820ac9df-49d1-590e-bc4c-409a7349c4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540749} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c67a319-c85c-5918-8f19-cbfe5ceda96a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ae3570d-9f53-5028-8c3b-4778adbb6463" :question/math-academy-id "q-352642" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x$ and $y=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.$ Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x & =-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+5x & =-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+5x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/d1/d19b70836b7b377c498fa505868563115706b588b0a2216d09c6ec738f746bbd.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]-[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\frac{32}{5}+8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
